1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,300
Welcome to Xmip.

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:07,230
Xmip stands for Cross OS Messaging & Integration Platform.

3
00:00:07,410 --> 00:00:12,385
It is an open-source messaging and integration platform written in Rust.

4
00:00:12,565 --> 00:00:19,140
It is on premises first and cloud installable, so organizations choose where their integration runs.

5
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:27,145
This is the Xmip project story: what it connects, how it records responsibility, and how people design and operate it.

6
00:00:29,325 --> 00:00:36,750
Xmip is being developed for organizations that need dependable messaging and integration under their own control.

7
00:00:36,930 --> 00:00:44,430
It carries forward familiar integration concepts while putting deployment and data under organizational control.

8
00:00:44,610 --> 00:00:53,510
Its central promise is accountability: once a message is accepted, its journey and outcome give operators evidence they can act on.

9
00:00:55,690 --> 00:01:01,465
For a CEO, the question is what happens to the business when a system exchange stops.

10
00:01:01,645 --> 00:01:07,070
Xmip focuses on accountable outcomes, recovery and organizational choice.

11
00:01:07,250 --> 00:01:14,225
A practical evaluation connects those principles to a real process, its owner and its business impact.

12
00:01:16,405 --> 00:01:21,605
For a CIO, Xmip keeps infrastructure decisions within the organization.

13
00:01:21,785 --> 00:01:28,185
Nodes can be placed close to systems, at remote sites or on chosen hosting infrastructure.

14
00:01:28,365 --> 00:01:36,265
Open implementation and recorded architecture make it possible to assess the platform and understand how it evolves.

15
00:01:38,445 --> 00:01:42,270
Consider an order from placement to confirmed delivery.

16
00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:47,300
The sales system sends the order as a Stream, and Xmip accepts a Message.

17
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:53,905
The payment system confirms money received, or an accepted promise to pay under the agreed terms.

18
00:01:54,085 --> 00:01:59,985
A Work Process coordinates the business steps and waits for the responses the order needs.

19
00:02:00,165 --> 00:02:06,765
If goods must be made, the factory receives a production request and returns progress and completion.

20
00:02:06,945 --> 00:02:11,770
If goods are in stock, the warehouse reserves, picks and packs them instead.

21
00:02:11,950 --> 00:02:17,800
When the goods are ready, the shipping system arranges dispatch and returns the shipment reference.

22
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:22,180
Carrier updates then report collection, transit and delivery.

23
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:30,235
A delay or failed delivery becomes a visible exception for follow-up, while a delivery confirmation completes the flow.

24
00:02:30,415 --> 00:02:38,190
Correlation connects the responses to the right order, and recorded state preserves progress across waits and restarts.

25
00:02:38,370 --> 00:02:43,420
That is orchestration: one order, many systems, and a visible outcome.

26
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:52,900
Before an order enters the business flow, the caller must follow the transport protocol and the configured Contract.

27
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:55,055
Otherwise the rule is clear.

28
00:02:55,235 --> 00:02:56,310
Stop.

29
00:02:56,490 --> 00:02:58,540
This request is refused.

30
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:06,970
Malformed protocol traffic, or content that violates the required Contract, must not be forwarded as a valid order.

31
00:03:07,150 --> 00:03:12,500
A refusal is audited, with a response to the caller where the protocol permits.

32
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:14,980
The sender must correct the request.

33
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:22,335
Required validation must never be silently bypassed: if it cannot run, the Location must not start.

34
00:03:22,515 --> 00:03:28,215
After acceptance, Xmip takes responsibility for the Message and its recorded progress.

35
00:03:28,395 --> 00:03:33,770
A refusal at the boundary is different from a delivery failure later in the Journey.

36
00:03:35,950 --> 00:03:40,250
A subscription expresses which messages a destination wants.

37
00:03:40,430 --> 00:03:46,080
Each match opens a journey, while an unmatched message remains available for replay.

38
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:54,085
Operators can pause a subscription without discarding its work, then resume the held messages in the order they arrived.

39
00:03:56,265 --> 00:03:58,515
Recovery starts with the outcome.

40
00:03:58,695 --> 00:04:01,845
A routing issue calls for correction and replay.

41
00:04:02,025 --> 00:04:08,350
A failed delivery calls for investigation and retry, or an intentional dismissal.

42
00:04:08,530 --> 00:04:15,605
Xmip keeps those actions distinct, while configured attempts and backoff handle repeated delivery attempts.

43
00:04:17,785 --> 00:04:20,760
In Xmip, an integration is one path.

44
00:04:20,940 --> 00:04:25,965
A Receive Location accepts the stream and its Receive Port publishes it.

45
00:04:26,145 --> 00:04:33,920
Subscriptions pick it up and take it, through a Work Process where the route has one, to a Send Port and its Send Location.

46
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:36,275
The path only runs forward.

47
00:04:36,455 --> 00:04:43,755
Replay starts a new journey from an earlier point, linked to the original, and every step writes its audit record.

48
00:04:45,935 --> 00:04:52,435
In Visual Studio Code, text editing and graphical designers work with the same configuration.

49
00:04:52,615 --> 00:05:03,215
Completion, validation and reference-aware renaming help developers make consistent changes, while Xmip’s language server supplies the rules behind the design.

50
00:05:05,395 --> 00:05:11,720
Xmip configuration uses readable TOML that teams can version, compare and review.

51
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:14,425
The designer works with that same source.

52
00:05:14,605 --> 00:05:21,155
This makes configuration an engineering artifact the team can understand and maintain together.

53
00:05:23,335 --> 00:05:27,385
Some exchanges need business steps as well as delivery.

54
00:05:27,565 --> 00:05:34,790
Xmip models these as Work Processes, designed in BPMN and compiled into executable units.

55
00:05:34,970 --> 00:05:39,620
Their state belongs to the ledger, so waiting work does not hold a thread.

56
00:05:41,800 --> 00:05:48,100
Xmip separates how data travels, how it is represented, and what the integration does with it.

57
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:53,630
Contracts define acceptance rules, while processing expresses business flow.

58
00:05:53,810 --> 00:06:00,635
Providers extend individual capabilities through explicit interfaces and a versioned C boundary.

59
00:06:00,815 --> 00:06:07,365
That modular architecture lets the platform evolve without treating every change as a replacement.

60
00:06:09,545 --> 00:06:15,245
Xmip carries Streams across its transport technologies, from business systems to the edge.

61
00:06:15,425 --> 00:06:20,225
A Stream is immutable data received by or produced within Xmip.

62
00:06:20,405 --> 00:06:29,055
SFTP, HTTP, AMQP, Kafka, PostgreSQL and OPC UA are examples of transport choices.

63
00:06:29,235 --> 00:06:36,210
A site build selects the technologies it needs, with availability demonstrated through each module's tests.

64
00:06:38,390 --> 00:06:41,090
Trust is explicit at Xmip’s boundaries.

65
00:06:41,270 --> 00:06:49,570
Parties carry identities, receive locations define what they accept, and outbound exchanges use their own identity.

66
00:06:49,750 --> 00:06:58,625
Certificate authentication, protected storage connections and encrypted embedded data give that architecture concrete foundations.

67
00:07:00,805 --> 00:07:03,880
Xmip Storage gives work a persistent home.

68
00:07:04,060 --> 00:07:11,035
A small deployment can host storage within its own node, while other nodes reach it over mutual TLS.

69
00:07:11,215 --> 00:07:17,915
The ledger holds messages, journeys and pending work, separate from administration and audit concerns.

70
00:07:20,095 --> 00:07:24,195
A deployment is a composition of roles and capabilities.

71
00:07:24,375 --> 00:07:32,925
Executing brings the message stages together in one process, while other roles serve storage, observation and operation.

72
00:07:33,105 --> 00:07:39,555
Site builds select the target and technologies; configuration determines what the node starts.

73
00:07:41,735 --> 00:07:47,810
Xmip’s architecture targets Windows, Linux and Mac computers with one integration model.

74
00:07:47,990 --> 00:07:54,515
Site builds select the roles and technologies for a developer machine, an edge gateway or a server.

75
00:07:54,695 --> 00:07:59,220
Packaging and validation progress separately on each platform.

76
00:08:01,400 --> 00:08:04,800
Offline operation is part of Xmip’s design.

77
00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:11,555
Packages can be prepared where dependencies are reachable, then brought into disconnected environments.

78
00:08:11,735 --> 00:08:20,535
Internet access is an explicit capability, supporting sites where connectivity is restricted, intermittent or deliberately absent.

79
00:08:22,715 --> 00:08:30,540
Operators can choose a command line, PowerShell, desktop or web interface without learning a different platform model.

80
00:08:30,720 --> 00:08:33,920
The same scopes and meanings guide investigation.

81
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:40,650
Automation and visual inspection become different ways to work with the same operational evidence.

82
00:08:42,830 --> 00:08:49,980
Xmip’s mobile host brings the same cluster health and scope navigation to Android and iPhone targets.

83
00:08:50,160 --> 00:08:53,610
It follows nodes remotely in the Observer role.

84
00:08:53,790 --> 00:08:57,090
Understand the affected scope wherever you are.

85
00:08:59,270 --> 00:09:02,745
Health should tell an operator where to look and what to do.

86
00:09:02,925 --> 00:09:06,575
Xmip uses named conditions and scope navigation.

87
00:09:06,755 --> 00:09:15,980
A parent holding points into its children, where the affected resource and its reason help distinguish a deliberate pause from strain or failure.

88
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:22,110
Current observation and audit answer different questions.

89
00:09:22,290 --> 00:09:25,290
Observation shows what is happening now.

90
00:09:25,470 --> 00:09:28,920
Audit records actions and outcomes over time.

91
00:09:29,100 --> 00:09:39,375
Chained program records, verification and journey evidence help the team investigate a specific exchange instead of guessing from disconnected symptoms.

92
00:09:41,555 --> 00:09:46,380
Xmip Events let other programs react to what happens in an integration.

93
00:09:46,560 --> 00:09:52,710
Subscribers select the outcomes and scopes they need, under the identity of a proven Party.

94
00:09:52,890 --> 00:09:59,990
The same Event model supports programs through the C interface and delivery over the wire through Xmip transports.

95
00:10:00,170 --> 00:10:05,620
Events carry references and metadata, keeping the business payload in its own place.

96
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:09,675
Subscriptions and deliveries are authorized and audited.

97
00:10:09,855 --> 00:10:15,805
This gives applications a common way to follow activity and build reactions around it.

98
00:10:17,985 --> 00:10:23,510
Retention and Archiving give Messages and their Streams a deliberate lifecycle.

99
00:10:23,690 --> 00:10:31,515
Retention keeps a Message and its immutable Stream data available for inspection and replay during the configured window.

100
00:10:31,695 --> 00:10:37,295
When that window passes, Archiving hands the retained data to the chosen archive.

101
00:10:37,475 --> 00:10:44,025
The selected archive stores the retained Streams and their metadata in its configured representation.

102
00:10:44,205 --> 00:10:49,180
The archive owner decides how long to keep it and when to dispose of it.

103
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:51,710
Xmip does not purge the archive.

104
00:10:51,890 --> 00:11:00,440
The capabilities are exercised in the Essentials; connecting the lifecycle to the assembled node remains an integration step.

105
00:11:02,620 --> 00:11:08,120
Publishing information to a data warehouse follows Xmip's Stream and Message model.

106
00:11:08,300 --> 00:11:12,275
A Subscription selects Messages for the analytics flow.

107
00:11:12,455 --> 00:11:22,730
A Work Process can transform their content into the target representation, and a Send Location delivers the resulting Stream through its configured transport.

108
00:11:22,910 --> 00:11:28,435
Another Subscription can serve the operational system from the same Publication.

109
00:11:28,615 --> 00:11:32,515
Teams define the warehouse mapping and loading behavior.

110
00:11:32,695 --> 00:11:37,570
The resulting Stream carries the information to the analytics destination.

111
00:11:37,750 --> 00:11:45,250
Parquet archiving provides a separate handoff of retained Stream bytes and metadata for downstream processing.

112
00:11:47,430 --> 00:11:50,430
Xmip brings Reports together in one list.

113
00:11:50,610 --> 00:11:54,785
Some present data that already exists, such as audit records.

114
00:11:54,965 --> 00:12:00,465
Others present findings from a Quest, which probes an installation without changing it.

115
00:12:00,645 --> 00:12:09,020
The Regulation Quest takes the regulation as a parameter, with GDPR first and an open set for additional modules.

116
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:15,900
Its Report shows a summary, a row for each requirement, and the evidence or gap behind that row.

117
00:12:16,080 --> 00:12:21,030
Gaps include proposed actions, which the operator chooses and applies.

118
00:12:21,210 --> 00:12:23,710
Xmip supplies evidence for review.

119
00:12:23,890 --> 00:12:27,590
The customer makes the legal judgment about compliance.

120
00:12:29,770 --> 00:12:35,045
Xmip also exposes read-only access through the Model Context Protocol.

121
00:12:35,225 --> 00:12:41,275
Clients can inspect health, measurements, audit and journeys through tools and scoped resources.

122
00:12:41,455 --> 00:12:50,280
The behavior follows the command line, and each answer is audited, keeping observation connected to established operational controls.

123
00:12:52,460 --> 00:12:57,285
Xmip is open source, so teams can inspect, improve and extend it.

124
00:12:57,465 --> 00:13:04,340
The platform is licensed under the GNU Affero General Public License, version three or later.

125
00:13:04,520 --> 00:13:10,120
Recorded architecture and explicit capability interfaces support that freedom.

126
00:13:10,300 --> 00:13:17,175
Independent modules loaded across the documented boundary can use their publisher’s chosen license.

127
00:13:19,355 --> 00:13:24,705
The engineering approach connects decisions to implementation and verification.

128
00:13:24,885 --> 00:13:33,160
Capability boundaries make ownership clear, while the Essentials and test harnesses exercise behavior and expose evidence.

129
00:13:33,340 --> 00:13:40,915
For the project team, progress means a demonstrated flow and reproducible results, alongside the design.

130
00:13:43,095 --> 00:13:48,445
Xmip uses two names to distinguish how the project evolves and how it is released.

131
00:13:48,625 --> 00:13:54,575
Continuum is the continuously evolving project stream, represented by the main branch.

132
00:13:54,755 --> 00:13:59,680
It holds the accepted architecture, documentation, tests and code.

133
00:13:59,860 --> 00:14:05,210
Linear is a stabilized release line derived from a specific Continuum state.

134
00:14:05,390 --> 00:14:10,440
A Linear release is versioned, tested, documented and reproducible.

135
00:14:12,620 --> 00:14:22,570
Xmip brings the story together: connect systems under your control, define the exchange in readable configuration, and follow its recorded outcome.

136
00:14:22,750 --> 00:14:26,900
Leaders assess business continuity and infrastructure choice.

137
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:30,955
Developers work in TOML and Visual Studio Code.

138
00:14:31,135 --> 00:14:37,360
Operators follow health, messages and journeys to investigate and recover the exchange.

139
00:14:37,540 --> 00:14:39,115
This is Xmip.

140
00:14:39,295 --> 00:14:43,045
Built to connect systems and record responsibility.
